## Row-Major Traversal
Visit every cell: outer loop over rows `i` from 0 to `m-1`, inner loop over columns `j` from 0 to `n-1`. Standard for reading or writing every cell.

## Column-Major Traversal
Outer loop over columns, inner over rows. Useful when processing the matrix column by column.

## Diagonal Traversal
Main diagonal (top-left to bottom-right): all cells where `i === j`. Anti-diagonal: all cells where `i + j === n - 1` (for a square matrix of side `n`). To iterate all cells on a given diagonal, fix `i - j` (main diagonals) or `i + j` (anti-diagonals) and collect cells with that value.

## Spiral Order
Maintain four boundaries: `top`, `bottom`, `left`, `right`. Traverse right across top row, down the right column, left across bottom row, up the left column. Shrink the corresponding boundary after each pass. Repeat while `top <= bottom && left <= right`.

```javascript
const result = [];
let top = 0, bottom = m - 1, left = 0, right = n - 1;
while (top <= bottom && left <= right) {
  for (let j = left; j <= right; j++) result.push(matrix[top][j]);
  top++;
  for (let i = top; i <= bottom; i++) result.push(matrix[i][right]);
  right--;
  if (top <= bottom)
    for (let j = right; j >= left; j--) result.push(matrix[bottom][j]);
  bottom--;
  if (left <= right)
    for (let i = bottom; i >= top; i--) result.push(matrix[i][left]);
  left++;
}
```

## Transpose
**Transposing** a matrix swaps `matrix[i][j]` with `matrix[j][i]`. Only iterate the upper triangle to avoid double-swapping:

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < n; i++)
  for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++)
    [matrix[i][j], matrix[j][i]] = [matrix[j][i], matrix[i][j]];
```

## Rotate 90° Clockwise
A 90° clockwise rotation equals: **transpose** then **reverse each row**. To see why geometrically: the top row of the original becomes the rightmost column after rotation. Transposing makes it the top row of the transposed matrix; reversing each row then moves it to the right.

```javascript
// Step 1: transpose
transpose(matrix);
// Step 2: reverse each row
for (const row of matrix) row.reverse();
```

## Rotate 90° Counter-Clockwise
Reverse each row first, then transpose. Or transpose then reverse each column.

**2D prefix sum** `pre[i][j]` stores the sum of all elements in the rectangle from `(0,0)` to `(i-1, j-1)`. Any sub-rectangle sum is answered in O(1) using inclusion-exclusion:

`sum(r1, c1, r2, c2) = pre[r2+1][c2+1] - pre[r1][c2+1] - pre[r2+1][c1] + pre[r1][c1]`

## Construction

```javascript
const pre = Array.from({ length: m + 1 }, () => new Array(n + 1).fill(0));
for (let i = 1; i <= m; i++)
  for (let j = 1; j <= n; j++)
    pre[i][j] = matrix[i-1][j-1] + pre[i-1][j] + pre[i][j-1] - pre[i-1][j-1];
```

The inclusion-exclusion formula avoids double-counting the top-left overlap rectangle that both `pre[i-1][j]` and `pre[i][j-1]` include.

A grid can be treated as an **implicit graph**: each cell `(r, c)` is a node, and edges connect it to its 4 (or 8) neighbors. BFS from a source cell visits cells in order of distance - useful for shortest path. DFS explores connected regions - useful for flood fill and island counting.

```javascript
const dirs = [[0,1],[0,-1],[1,0],[-1,0]]; // right, left, down, up

function bfs(grid, startR, startC) {
  const m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
  const visited = Array.from({ length: m }, () => new Array(n).fill(false));
  const queue = [[startR, startC]];
  visited[startR][startC] = true;
  while (queue.length) {
    const [r, c] = queue.shift();
    for (const [dr, dc] of dirs) {
      const nr = r + dr, nc = c + dc;
      if (nr >= 0 && nr < m && nc >= 0 && nc < n && !visited[nr][nc]) {
        visited[nr][nc] = true; // mark BEFORE enqueuing
        queue.push([nr, nc]);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

For DFS, replace the queue with a recursive call or explicit stack.

